      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Tests of this kind usually record relative humidity, airborne particulates and carbon monoxide. Many also capture volatile organic compounds and carbon dioxide where the instrument set includes those channels. A Chicago basement can read damp in August while a top-floor bedroom reads far drier in the same hour. That spread is the finding. City & Suburban sends NATE-certified technicians, so each number is read against the furnace, coil and ducts behind it."
